 this debate that we're  having now a real kind of  anxiety and the part of some Americans  that you know that our heritage is is  really changing now you mentioned  something interesting that I think is  important to point out throughout most  of our history there hasn't really been  much debate around this notion that we  are a nation of immigrants now we are  actually seeing a debate about that  there are voices on that largely on  their right who are saying you know  we're not really a nation of immigrants  who are a nation of settlers and they're  distinguished distinguishing between  immigrants and settlers or pioneers in  the sense that settlers and pioneers  built the country and then immigrants  seen the United States already built  already powerful already prosperous  wanted to come in and take advantage of  that and so there's this distinction  between immigrants and settlers which is  something that what I never came across  that ever before in the history of our  country yes so like as a correspondent  who covers this stuff when you hear that  brought up you've not heard this  distinction before between settlers and  immigrants and now we're hearing it or  with any of the other distinctions that  are being made yes immigration before  was is different than immigration now or  any of these distinction  how can you examine on as close to an  objective basis as possible whether what  underlies that is or maybe racism or  xenophobia as some suggest or whether  the defense that no we're talking here  about a country that has limited  resources and at a certain point  decisions have to be made about who gets  to come in or who doesn't or the idea of  losing status or the driving down of  wages as the talking point goes how can  you as a reporter say okay I'm hearing  the assertion let's now examine what  really is underlying it that's a really  astute question David and I would say  that the the important change or the  distinction to be made is between those  who are nervous about the numbers of  immigrants coming in and fearing  therefore that you know the sheer  quantity of immigration could jeopardize  you know job prospects for Nate  born Americans that is one concern  that's a concern about numbers and then  there is a concern about culture which  really has nothing to do with the  numbers of immigrants coming in it has  to do with the kind of immigrants who  are coming in that's a very different  argument though those are the arguments  that emphasize sort of a threat to our  our heritage our Western traditions our  civilization it's a it's a it's an  argument about identity about culture I  think those are two very different  arguments the the first one I think you  would have a hard time arguing that that  is essentially a racist argument it's a  kind of a economic argument it can be  debated both ways the second one because  it emphasizes cultural differences I  think that's a lot more problematic are  the lines clear in your mind in terms of  the parties the two major parties and  this issue and to add some context to  that the discussion around the shutdown  in the wall is mostly dividing among  party lines so you have people on the  Left who tend to be against the wall and  people on the right who all else being  equal are generally more in favor of the  wall proposed by the president but if  you look a little bit wider like the  last 30 years you had a Republican in  Ronald Reagan who was granted some  degree of amnesty through to Trump who  wants to build this wall and is talking  about [ __ ] countries even on the  Democratic side you had deportation  records set by President Barack Obama  and now this push towards some in some  areas sanctuary cities and opposition to  the wall so to get back to the question  are the lines as clear as they might  seem in terms of party and this issue  they've really changed David there is a  and a strong argument to be made that  the US economy depends on immigrant  lab